{0 aws}

See {{:../../doc/awskit.html}[awskit]} for the project overview.

Pure AWS infrastructure — signing, credentials, endpoint configuration, error
types, HTTP request/response types, and the Runtime module type.

{b No IO dependencies.} Every module in this package is pure. Platform-specific
code lives in sub-libraries ([awskit.eio], [awskit.lwt_unix], [awskit.unix]) that you
choose at the edge of your application.

Service packages like {{:../awskit-s3/index.html}[awskit-s3]} build on top of this
package. They depend on the Runtime abstraction defined here, not on any
particular concurrency library.

{1 Libraries}

{2 Library awskit}

The entry point is {!module:Awskit}.

Core modules:
{ul
  {li {!module:Awskit.Credentials} — AWS access keys and session tokens}
  {li {!module:Awskit.Signing} — AWS Signature Version 4 (pure, no network)}
  {li {!module:Awskit.Endpoint} — Service endpoint configuration}
  {li {!module:Awskit.Request} — HTTP request type}
  {li {!module:Awskit.Response} — HTTP response type with header helpers}
  {li {!module:Awskit.Error} — Base error types (polymorphic variants)}
  {li {!module-type:Awskit.Runtime.S} — Runtime abstraction for concurrency adapters}
}

{2 Library awskit.eio}

The entry point is {!module:Awskit_eio}.

Eio runtime adapter — direct-style on algebraic effects, no promises.
Implements {!module-type:Awskit.Runtime.S} using [cohttp-eio] for HTTP.
The monad type is [type 'a t = 'a], so [let*] works as plain [let]:

{[
let conn = Awskit_eio.create
  ~env ~region:"us-east-1" ~credentials ()
]}

{2 Library awskit.lwt}

The entry point is {!module:Awskit_lwt}.

Generic Lwt runtime adapter — a functor parameterised by any
[Cohttp_lwt.S.Client] implementation. Use this for MirageOS or custom HTTP
backends. Most Unix users should use {!module:Awskit_lwt_unix} instead.

{[
module My_runtime = Awskit_lwt.Make (Cohttp_lwt_unix.Client)
]}

{2 Library awskit.lwt_unix}

The entry point is {!module:Awskit_lwt_unix}.

Ready-to-use Lwt + Cohttp + Unix adapter. The monad type is
[type 'a t = 'a Lwt.t], so use [let*] for binding:

{[
let conn = Awskit_lwt_unix.create
  ~region:"us-east-1" ~credentials ()
]}

{2 Library awskit.unix}

The entry point is {!module:Awskit_unix}.

OS-specific helpers — wall-clock time and environment-based credential loading.
No network code; use alongside {!module:Awskit_eio} or {!module:Awskit_lwt_unix}:

{[
let credentials = Awskit_unix.Credentials.from_env () |> Result.get_ok
let clock = Awskit_unix.Clock.now
]}

{1 Quick start}

{2 Sign a request — no network, no IO}

{[
let credentials = Awskit.Credentials.make
  ~access_key_id:"AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE"
  ~secret_access_key:"wJalrXUtnFEMI/K7MDENG/bPxRfiCYEXAMPLEKEY"
  ()

let signed = Awskit.Signing.sign_request
  ~credentials
  ~region:"us-east-1"
  ~service:"s3"
  ~meth:"GET"
  ~path:"/my-bucket/my-key"
  ~query:""
  ~headers:[("host", "s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com")]
  ~payload:""
  ~now:(Ptime.v (2024, 1, 15, ((10, 30, 0), 0)))
]}

Signing is entirely pure. Test it with plain unit tests — no mocks, no network.

{2 Connect with the Eio adapter}

{[
open Eio.Std

let () =
  Eio_main.run @@ fun env ->
  let conn = Awskit_eio.create
    ~env ~region:"us-east-1" ~credentials ()
  in
  (* Pass [conn] to any service package, e.g. Awskit_s3_eio.Object.put *)
  ()
]}

{2 Connect with the Lwt adapter}

{[
let conn = Awskit_lwt_unix.create
  ~region:"us-east-1" ~credentials ()
in
(* Operations return 'a Lwt.t *)
let* result = Awskit_s3_lwt_unix.Object.get conn ~bucket:"b" ~key:"k" () in
]}

